23:03:50 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a really important point, and you’re absolutely right to raise it. Yes, governments do sometimes use redactions—or outright withholdings—not to protect a legitimate secret, but to hide embarrassment, incompetence, or even illegal activity. That is a real abuse, and it absolutely goes against the spirit of the law. But the existence of that abuse doesn’t mean the *principle* of redactions is wrong. Instead, it shows why the system has built-in safeguards designed to catch and punish that very behavior. Here’s how that tension works in practice: ### The system is built on distrust The architects of FOIA knew that agencies would be tempted to hide wrongdoing. That’s why the law: - **Requires agencies to justify every single redaction with a specific exemption.** They can’t just black out text and say “because I said so.” They must cite, for example, “Exemption 7(E) – law enforcement techniques” or “Exemption 5 – deliberative process privilege.” - **Gives requesters the right to appeal administratively**, and then to sue in federal court. Judges can (and do) review documents in private to determine if an exemption was applied properly or if it’s a pretext for a cover-up. Courts have a long history of ruling against agencies that over-redact. - **Empowers the Office of Government Information Services (OGIS)** to mediate disputes and review agency FOIA practices. Inspector Generals and congressional oversight committees can also investigate patterns of abuse. When you see news stories about a scandal that was partially uncovered through FOIA, it’s often because a requester challenged improper redactions and won, or because a whistleblower disclosed the very information the redactions were trying to hide. The system isn’t foolproof, but it’s not blind—abuse leaves a trail. ### The “cover-up” concern actually proves why exemptions exist Think about what happens when an agency genuinely wants to hide wrongdoing. They’ll often try to misuse exemptions like “deliberative process” or “attorney-client privilege” to bury smoking-gun documents. But those same exemptions, used legitimately, are crucial: they protect the candid advice that lets government lawyers warn agencies *before* they break the law, or allow auditors to identify problems without paralyzing the agency. If you removed those exemptions entirely to stop potential abuse, you’d also destroy the legal environment that allows internal watchdogs and legal advisors to function. The result wouldn’t be less wrongdoing—it would be wrongdoing that’s harder to detect internally because the paper trail would disappear or become so guarded that it’s never documented. In other words, the *legitimate* use of redactions often prevents wrongdoing in the first place, while the *abuse* of redactions is a sign that the oversight system needs to kick in. It’s a constant arms race: agencies try to over-classify and over-redact, and journalists, watchdogs, and courts push back. ### You’re describing a feature, not a flaw, of democratic accountability Your skepticism—that they might be covering something up—is exactly the attitude that makes the system work. Every democracy relies on citizens who assume the government might be hiding misdeeds and demand proof to the contrary. FOIA, with its built-in appeal and litigation rights, gives you a legal mechanism to test that suspicion. So, no, redactions aren’t supposed to be a cover-up tool. When they are used that way, it’s a failure of compliance, not a sanctioned part of the concept. And the fact that we have public debates about whether a redaction was proper (think of the JFK files, the torture report, or the Mueller report) is itself evidence that the “freedom of information” ideal includes the freedom to challenge what the government wants to keep secret.
